<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Global Work – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> International English</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> How English Is Used in Workplaces Globally</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Take basic notes on a text about a familiar topic. Reading: Recognize the writer's point of view in various texts.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can take introductory notes on articles about English as a global language, recording main ideas (e.g., "English connects people from different cultures in business")
Can recognize a writer's viewpoint on the spread of English, noting arguments about its importance in international business (e.g., "The article highlights that sectors like logistics and finance in Panama rely heavily on English for communication.")
Can summarize points from texts on English in the workplace, preparing for discussions on language learning (e.g., "The text argues that English skills can enhance career opportunities.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #e0e7ee; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #e0e7ee; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #e0e7ee; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage aims to engage students, activate prior knowledge about English in workplaces, and model target language features relevant to reading comprehension and pronunciation. It sets the context for the upcoming reading task.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a world map or images of people working in various global industries (e.g., IT, tourism, logistics). Students are asked to brainstorm where and how English might be used in these contexts. Examples: "English connects people from different cultures in business." Students share their ideas in pairs. (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary from the previous lesson and upcoming text, focusing on words with the long vowel /e/ sound (e.g., "relevant," "necessary," "employees") and reduced vowels in multisyllabic words (e.g., "communication," "accessible"). Teacher writes these words on the board and underlines the target sounds, repeating them clearly. (3 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is English always necessary for local jobs?" (Expected answer: "No, but it could be important for international ones.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'cross-cultural communication,' what does that mean?" (Expected answer: "It means communicating with people from different countries or backgrounds.")</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher checks if students recall previous vocabulary like "lingua franca" or "global interactions." (2 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will engage with an authentic text to understand the main ideas and specific details about English in global workplaces. This stage introduces the core reading material and guides initial comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> Students receive a short article excerpt titled "The Vital Role of English in Global Business Success." The text discusses why English has become an international language, highlighting its use in various sectors like logistics and finance, and the benefits of English fluency for employees.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Material:</b> Authentic article excerpt (B1.2 level) from a business or career advice blog/website. The register is formal yet accessible. Printed handouts or projected text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding written texts for specific information and identifying the writer's perspective on the importance of English in international business.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the article once quickly. Teacher asks: "What is the main idea of this article?" and "What is the author's general opinion about English in the workplace?" (e.g., "The article highlights that sectors like logistics and finance in Panama rely heavily on English for communication.") (3 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> Students read the text again to determine if 4-5 statements are true or false based on the information provided. (e.g., "English is only important in tech companies. T/F"). This prompts specific detail retrieval. (4 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Match key vocabulary words (e.g., "fluency," "cross-cultural communication," "enhance") from the text to their definitions or synonyms. (3 min)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on developing accuracy in reading comprehension, note-taking, and recognizing specific language structures and vocabulary from the text. Students practice identifying key information and the writer's point of view.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Focusing on taking basic notes on a text, recognizing writer's point of view, and identifying examples of Present Perfect, Modals (must, could, should, can), and target vocabulary (global, international, communication, skills, fluency, enhance).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Guided Note-taking:</b> The teacher provides a graphic organizer or a template for note-taking. Students re-read the article, highlighting or underlining sentences that convey main ideas in each paragraph. They then practice taking basic notes, recording these main ideas (e.g., "English has become an international language, widely used in business to help people communicate.") and identifying specific arguments about its importance in international business. (5 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Grammar and Vocabulary Spot Check:</b> In pairs, students identify sentences in the text that use the Present Perfect (e.g., "English has become...") and Modals (e.g., "It could be a good idea to gain experience..."). They also identify 3-4 key vocabulary words and explain their meaning in the context of the article. Teacher circulates, providing support and correcting pronunciation if needed, especially for words like "relevant" or "necessary." (5 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Identifying Writer's Viewpoint:</b> Students are given a set of statements, some reflecting the writer's viewpoint from the article, and others presenting contrasting views. They must identify which statements truly represent the writer's point of view, providing textual evidence. For example, "The author believes English skills can enhance career opportunities. Do you agree based on the text?" (5 min)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 15 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will use the information and notes gathered from the reading to engage in a collaborative task. This stage emphasizes the application of reading comprehension skills and the ability to summarize and articulate learned information in a realistic scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative task:</b> "Career Advice Column Response"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4). Each group imagines they are writing a response to a 'reader's question' in a career advice column, based *only* on the information from the article they read. The reader's question is: "I'm a student wondering about my future career. Why has English become such an important language for international business, and how can I prepare?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative written summary/response (approx. 50-70 words) or a brief oral presentation of their collective notes and summary. They must prepare to summarize points from texts on English in the workplace, preparing for discussions on language learning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme:</b> This task directly addresses "How English Is Used in Workplaces Globally" and requires them to synthesize information from the reading. They will use phrases like "Practicing English helps in cross-cultural workplaces" and "Effective communication skills help us succeed globally."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in discussing and summarizing information from the text, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion. Students should incorporate target vocabulary (e.g., "global," "communication," "skills," "enhance") and grammar (Present Perfect, Modals) naturally in their responses. They could also use indirect questions when formulating advice (e.g., "Can you explain which skills can help you advance your career?").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage evaluates students' ability to comprehend the text, take notes, and articulate their understanding, while also providing an opportunity for self-reflection on their learning process.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on students' participation in the group discussion/summary activity and review of their submitted notes/summary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their individual notes from Stage 3 and their group's summary response from Stage 4. This allows assessment of both individual comprehension and collaborative application.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and accuracy of notes (identification of main ideas and supporting details).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognition of the writer's point of view on English's importance.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4ph; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., "global," "communication," "enhance") and grammatical structures (Present Perfect, Modals) in the summary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Coherence and completeness of the group's summary response, directly addressing the reader's question based on the text.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can (circle one: easily / somewhat / with difficulty) take notes on main ideas from an article."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new idea I learned about English in global workplaces today is..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 8 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This final stage encourages learners and the teacher to reflect on the lesson's effectiveness, identify areas for growth, and bridge the learning to the next lesson in the sequence.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Student Self-Reflection:</b> Learners answer two guiding questions:</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of reading and understanding the article today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How will improving my reading skills, especially for workplace-related texts, help me in the future?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What worked well in helping students take notes and recognize the writer's viewpoint? The structured note-taking template seemed effective."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next reading lesson? Perhaps more explicit modeling of identifying subjective language for writer's viewpoint."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Were the students able to correctly pronounce the target vowel sounds and reduced vowels? More attention needed for 'accessible' vs 'access'."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Connection to next lesson:</b> The information and notes gathered from this reading lesson will serve as a foundation for Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction). Students will use their understanding of English's role in global workplaces and their notes to discuss and express opinions in interactive tasks, applying phrases like "Why has English become such an important language for international business?" and "It's important to adapt language for different audiences."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 7 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>